{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., one where a larger portion of the probability mass is on unfavorable outcomes, typically many small losses and a small chance of a large gain). To evaluate skewness, we need to compare the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A offers 3000€ with 0.2% probability and 0€ with 99.8%. Lottery B offers 6000€ with 0.1% probability and 0€ with 99.9%. Both lotteries have a single positive outcome and a high probability of zero, which is typical of right-skewed distributions. To assess skewness: Lottery A has twice the probability of winning but half the prize of Lottery B. In terms of outcome distribution, Lottery B assigns even more probability to the zero (unfavorable) outcome than A (99.9% vs 99.8%) and has a smaller chance of payout (0.1% vs 0.2%), but the payout is larger. Thus, Lottery B is more right-skewed than Lottery A because it concentrates even more probability on the unfavorable (zero) outcome while offering a rarer but larger gain. The decision reason would therefore prefer Lottery B, as it is more right-skewed.",
  "step_2": "The individual acknowledges that the probabilities of winning are extremely low in both cases, so they expect to win 0€ regardless. They state that since losses are not possible, they prefer to 'challenge fate' and go for the higher potential gain (6000€) in the unlikely event of winning. The justification centers on the desire for a higher upside in an already improbable win, despite the lower probability. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or any comparison of how probability mass is distributed across favorable vs unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the focus is on the magnitude of the prize in the case of an unlikely win and a willingness to take a 'gamble' for a bigger reward when there is no downside risk.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring more right-skewed lotteries, which in this case is Lottery B. The individual also chooses Lottery B (implied by preferring the 6000€ option), so the choice aligns with the reason. However, the justification is not based on skewness or the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the individual is motivated by the higher potential reward in the case of a win and a willingness to 'dare fate' when there is no downside. This reasoning is more aligned with risk-seeking behavior or a preference for maximum upside under near-zero probability events, rather than an assessment of skewness per se. The information considered by the individual (prize size, no downside, low probabilities) overlaps partially with the structural features of the lotteries, but the conceptual focus (skewness as imbalance in probability distribution) is absent. There is no indication that the individual evaluated how much probability is assigned to unfavorable vs favorable outcomes as a basis for preference. Therefore, while the choice matches what the reason would recommend, the reasoning process does not reflect the decision rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
