  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and preferring the more right-skewed lottery (B, in this case). However, the individual considers the probability of loss and the size of the loss, ultimately choosing based on the smaller loss amount. This aligns with a loss-aversion or minimax-type heuristic, not skewness-based reasoning. The information considered by the individual (loss size and similar probabilities) overlaps partially with the data needed for skewness (probabilities and outcomes), but the interpretation and decision rule are different. The individual does not assess the overall shape of the distribution or compare how probability mass is distributed across favorable vs unfavorable outcomes in a way that defines skewness. Instead, they focus on the worst possible outcome and prefer the less bad one. The justification is outcome-centered, not distribution-shape-centered. Therefore, there is no evidence that the skewness-based rule was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
