  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring more right-skewed lotteries (i.e., those with higher concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes). Lottery A is more right-skewed (90% on loss) than B (45% on loss), so the reason would favor A. However, the individual chooses B (or expresses preference for B) because they want to avoid the 'almost certain' loss in A. This indicates they are averse to high-probability losses and thus prefer less right-skewed options — the opposite of what the reason prescribes. The individual considers the probability and magnitude of losses, which overlaps partially with the inputs to skewness (probabilities and outcomes), but their justification is based on minimizing the chance of loss, not on favoring greater skewness. Their reasoning runs counter to the decision reason’s preference rule. Therefore, they did not use this reason; in fact, they acted against it.",
