{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., has a higher probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones). To evaluate this, we must assess the skewness of each lottery. Lottery A offers 500 Euros with 100% probability, which is a degenerate (non-random) outcome and thus has no skewness in the usual distributional sense—technically, it is symmetric. Lottery B offers 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. This distribution has a mean of 500 Euros, but half the time it yields 0 (an unfavorable outcome relative to the mean) and half the time 1000 (favorable). Since the unfavorable outcome (0) has equal probability to the favorable one (1000), it is symmetric, not right-skewed. Neither lottery is clearly right-skewed. However, if we define 'unfavorable' as below a subjective threshold (e.g., 0 being very bad), one might interpret Lottery B as right-skewed because it risks 0 with 50% chance. Still, by standard statistical definition, Lottery B is symmetric. The decision reason would prefer the more right-skewed lottery, but in this case, neither is clearly more right-skewed. Thus, the reason does not clearly prefer A or B. However, the reason is applicable in principle because skewness can be assessed from the probabilities and outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The individual describes the decision as difficult but ultimately chooses the certain 500€ (Lottery A). The information considered includes the emotional impact of outcomes: both 500€ and 1000€ are seen as beneficial and acceptable, but going home with 0€ would be very disappointing. The individual focuses on the certainty of 500€ versus the risk of getting nothing in Lottery B. There is no mention of skewness, probability distributions, or statistical properties like asymmetry. The justification is based on risk aversion and anticipated regret or disappointment from receiving 0€. The deliberation centers on emotional consequences and the psychological weight of losing (getting 0) rather than on structural features of the probability distribution such as skewness.",
