{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., has a higher probability mass on lower, unfavorable outcomes compared to higher, favorable ones). To assess skewness, we need to examine the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A has a 75% chance of 0€ and 25% chance of 6000€. Lottery B has a 50% chance of 0€, 25% of 4000€, and 25% of 2000€. Thus, Lottery A assigns 75% probability to the lowest outcome (0€), while Lottery B assigns only 50% to 0€. Therefore, Lottery A has a greater concentration of probability on the lowest outcome and is more right-skewed than Lottery B. The decision reason would prefer Lottery A because it is more right-skewed. The relevant information (probabilities and outcomes) is fully available, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The individual states that the decision is not very easy, but since both options only involve gains (or at least no losses, with 0€ still seen as a non-loss/gain), they choose the box that 'am ehesten über 0€ auszahlt' (most likely to pay out above 0€). This indicates the individual is focusing on the probability of receiving more than 0€. For Lottery A, the chance of getting more than 0€ is 25% (only the 6000€ outcome). For Lottery B, the chance of getting more than 0€ is 50% (25% for 4000€ + 25% for 2000€). Therefore, the individual is favoring Lottery B because it has a higher probability of yielding a positive payout. The justification is based on maximizing the chance of a non-zero gain, not on the shape of the distribution or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring more right-skewed lotteries (greater probability on unfavorable outcomes). Lottery A is more right-skewed (75% on 0€) and would be preferred by the reason. However, the individual chooses based on the likelihood of receiving more than 0€, which favors Lottery B. The information considered by the individual (probability of >0€ payout) differs from the information relevant to skewness (distribution shape, concentration on low vs high outcomes). Moreover, the individual’s justification (favoring higher chance of non-zero gain) contradicts the decision reason’s preference for more right-skewed (i.e., riskier in terms of higher chance of zero) lotteries. There is no mention or implication of considering distribution asymmetry or skewness. Therefore, the individual is using a different criterion (chance of positive outcome) than the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

